{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791384843,"nonce":"IRjC6A00rNVXtoV6I5PRlmLrsy4=","title":"HIR: reduce repeated dominator construction costs","message":"Shared optimizer passes construct DominatorTree repeatedly. The old immediate-dominator intersection walks hash maps for every step, while construction eagerly calculates dominance frontiers unused by production callers. Use dense RPO positions for the fixed point and compute frontiers lazily on query, retaining the analyzed join predecessors so queries do not depend on the function's later mutation.\n\nValidate against an independent path-removal oracle across cyclic CFGs and verify downstream optimizers and both frontends. Measure NUC LLVM-off startup and wall time against committed baseline 76bc7ca9bb0884e41b4b835e0cfe65dc3bfb707d. First screen improves several Lua wall times but regresses Python FFT 494 to 624 ms; investigate generated code and promotion before accepting. No performance claim yet.","files":null}]}